#!/bin/bash

# On an upgrade dpkg passes the previously installed version as $2, so the
# notice below is only shown to people coming from before the manifest format.
if [ "$1" = "configure" ] && [ -n "$2" ]; then
    if dpkg --compare-versions "$2" lt "4.0.0"; then
        echo ""
        echo "============================================================================="
        echo " ian 4.0.0 is a significant change from the version you were running ($2)."
        # quoted heredoc: the body is printed literally, no expansion
        cat <<'EOF'

 What goes into a package is now decided by DEBIAN/md5sums, not by sweeping
 the repo and applying .ianignore:

   * only files registered with "ian add" are packaged
   * .ianignore is no longer used
   * bare files in the repo root are no longer swept into usr/share/doc,
     they are registered explicitly with "ian doc"
   * "ian pkg" fails if a registered file no longer matches its md5 sum
   * the "ian excludes" command is gone

 Existing packages will not build until they are migrated. In each package:

   ian migrate      # shows what it would register, writes nothing
   ian migrate -f   # applies it, and deletes .ianignore

 Then review and commit DEBIAN/md5sums and DEBIAN/docfiles.
 "ian status" shows which registered files have drifted.
=============================================================================

EOF
    fi
fi

exit 0;
